{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00169 - MONDO_1068",
  "verbalization": "Cholecalciferol, also known as vitamin D3, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Osteomalacia (disease) through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Cholecalciferol is associated with Rickets (disease) and Renal Osteodystrophy (disease), both of which are linked to Osteomalacia (disease) via intermediate entities. This indicates that Cholecalciferol may influence calcium homeostasis and bone mineralization, which are critical for bone health. The presence of Calcifediol, a metabolite of Cholecalciferol, in the indication chain for Osteomalacia (disease) further supports the role of vitamin D in bone metabolism. The cumulative evidence suggests that Cholecalciferol may contribute to the development of Osteomalacia (disease) through its effects on calcium and phosphate regulation, potentially via the production of Calcifediol and its subsequent involvement in bone resorption and deposition. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of vitamin D in maintaining bone integrity, and the observed associations suggest that Cholecalciferol may be a candidate for repurposing in the treatment of Osteomalacia (disease).",
